Yeah, that's why I said with luck above 12 the missile dissappearance
chance worsen, but my math was off; it's actually above 9 that it
worsens.  The key is the "i -= (x <= 15 && Luck >= -5 ? Luck/3 :
Luck);
" line in rnl; although the chances of actually hitting that line are
slightly smaller (monotonically) as luck increases, its effect is
magnified when it hits (up until i=x-1, which is where I made my math
error thinking it was up to i=x).  The effect isn't that bad, though.

In practice, the latter factor dominates.  Running each luck value a
million times on my machine, I get the following chances of
"rnl(4)==0":
Luck 0: 0.250041
Luck 1: 0.250176
Luck 2: 0.250008
Luck 3: 0.494582
Luck 4: 0.494766
Luck 5: 0.494958
Luck 6: 0.739410
Luck 7: 0.739430
Luck 8: 0.739902
Luck 9: 0.985016
Luck 10: 0.984904
Luck 11: 0.985043
Luck 12: 0.985083
Luck 13: 0.984986
Luck 14: 0.985103

(it remains about 25% for luck of -1 or -2, and 0 for luck <-2).


